not that long ago we were a tiny little  startup with a crazy idea and here we  are getting ready to go public so that  was coinbase's ceo just a few weeks  before the company scored an 85 billion  valuation in its stock market debut  since it was founded in 2012 the company  has grown to be the largest  cryptocurrency exchange in the u.s  thanks in part to its focus on making  crypto accessible it's just going to be  sort of an easy  seamless way for people to just get into  crypto and trade it that was their idea  it helped the company bring in record  revenue in 2021 as more traders joined  the platform  but as bitcoin and other  cryptocurrencies fell coinbase's share  value fell along with it exposing deeper  issues with its business practices and  culture  so  what went wrong  in 2021 the crypto industry was booming  coinbase  public listing on its own was driving a  lot of enthusiasm for the rest of the  market this was seen as a bellwether  news event  the explosion in crypto drove more  people including many retail traders to  coinbase's platform and that drove up  revenue in a big way  most of the company's money comes from  trading fees which it charges two types  of traders first retail more basically  you know mom and pop young kids people  like you and me people just kind of  doing this out there on their own and  second institutional brokerage houses  pension funds hedge funds i mean people  who are out there who have a business  that is predicated on your trading  assets in the capital market much of  coinbase's trading volume comes from  these traders but most of their revenue  comes from these ones who make up its  core customer base in the second quarter  of 2021 90 of coinbase's 2 billion net  revenue was made from retail  transactions alone spurred on by a  growing user base coinbase set its  sights on growth they beefed up on  hiring aiming to catch up to competitors  that had a wider span of crypto assets  and products between the end of the  first and third quarters it added more  than a thousand employees in october  coinbase announced it would launch its  own nft marketplace and in november the  share price shot to a high point as the  value of bitcoin peaked but there was  trouble ahead especially for a company  that relied on crypto enthusiasm these  things are all lashed very very tightly  to sentiment in the crypto markets in  the stock market  stocks like coinbase become a proxy for  the crypto markets and that's why you  see the stock prices track crypto so  closely as the fed began to shift its  monetary policy to tackle inflation many  professional investors started to pull  out of assets that they considered  riskier crypto became sort of one asset  in a larger portfolio if you're a  professional trader you make you you buy  bonds you buy stocks you buy commodities  you buy currencies and now you were  buying crypto so you were slotting  crypto in among those different assets  and where it ended up being slotted for  most of them was it was a risk asset it  was a highly valid item in april  coinbase's share price dropped steeply  as trading volume fell and analysts  slashed estimates for earnings  transactions were down and retail  traders were pulling out of the market  some were huddling shorthand for holding  on for dear life it means that they were  sitting on their cryptocurrencies trying  to ride out price declines and hoping to  come out on top at the other end that  also means that they were making fewer  trades in transactions the main things  that coinbase relies on to make money  the stock price continued to slide in  may when the company reported losses in  the first quarter when you started to  see business results come in and the  business results were really bad i think  that's when people started to realize  that this was not just one of you know  crypto's sort of periodic common spasms  as trading continued to slow and revenue  fell larger problems within the company  were exposed  certainly early in 2022 it was hard to  tell how severe the sell-off was going  to be there wasn't a lot of panic and  you saw companies like coinbase they  kept hiring they were in expansion mode  and they just kept running with that  despite the fact that the prices started  to fall the company hadn't built up  other streams of revenue and time to  make up for falling profits the nft  marketplace they had announced in  october didn't materialize until april  just as the nft market was flatlining  the platform did just 4.2 million in  transactions between when it went live  in april and the beginning of august  coinbase has also offered things like  staking and custodial services now  especially it's just a really hard time  to try to build new businesses in the  middle of you know completely down  market  in early june a petition purportedly  from coinbase employees was posted to  another crypto site it called to oust  several executives over the company's  performance armstrong tweeted that the  petition was really dumb on multiple  levels i think probably issues that were  inside the company that largely were  tapered over because times were so good  in 2021 started to emerge in 2022.  a few weeks later the company announced  it would cut its workforce by about 18  to reduce spending its employee account  had grown to nearly 5 000 and coinbase  ran into more trouble in july as federal  authorities charged a former product  manager from the company with insider  trading lawyers for the former product  manager said that the charges were  meritless  the sec also took the position that  several of the crypto assets traded on  the company's platform are classified as  securities  the company which isn't licensed to  operate as a securities exchange denied  that the assets qualify  by the end of quarter two the retail  trading volume fell by 74 from the high  in quarter four of 2021.  to make up for lost ground coinbase  plans to continue to grow its other  streams of revenue  in june it launched a derivatives  product that allows traders to bet on  the price of an underlying asset the  company announced a partnership with  money management firm blackrock in  august which could help increase the  number of institutional investors that  use the platform  coinbase says that it has built up more  client requested products and is seeing  a strong uptake  armstrong told cnbc in august that the  company is planning on increasing its  earnings from subscriptions and services  which made up about 18 of its quarter  two revenue  we're realizing that trading fees is not  going to be that thing that you know  it's going to be it's still going to be  a major part of our business in 10 years  from now even 20 years from now but i'd  like to get to a place where more than  50 of our revenue is subscription and  services businesses across the crypto  industry have been impacted by the  downturn but competitors like binance  and ftx have continued to grow and are  gaining market share of spot trading  however since neither company is public  it's not clear how much their earnings  have currently been impacted by the  market  in its quarter two letter to  shareholders coinbase said that it lives  by the mantra it's never as good as it  seems and it's never as bad as it seems  and that it is an all-weather company  with experience in navigating crypto  asset price cycles  the company's share price has seen a  small increase in recent months coinbase  says that it continues to take a  long-term view and remains focused on  building for the future
 

